{"type":"thinking","text":"All four nametable addresses give identical full-ground rows. So the PPU nametable memory is mirrored such that all four reads hit the same physical memory (or the data is identical).\n\nHmm, given all 4 are identical, it's very likely the nametables are mirrored (only one distinct 1KB region). But then how does the game scroll? Well — the game can use one nametable plus the \"scroll\" and it draws the level into it in a 512px window... but with mirrored nametables it can still scroll by redrawing columns as it goes (each frame or during vblank), using the single nametable and the attribute data.\n\nHmm, actually SMB does use both nametables (it's known that SMB uses vertical mirroring... whatever).\n\nAnyway: the ground row is FULL everywhere in the nametable. So there is no visible pit in the nametable. Yet Mario falls.\n\n**CONCLUSION: Mario is NOT falling into a pit.** The $00CE increasing past 176 must mean something else!\n\nLet me reconsider. What if $00CE is not \"screen y\" but Mario's y in a coordinate where larger = lower, and beyond 176 means he's on a LOWER surface — like the flagpole area or a lower platform? But there's no lower platform.\n\nHmm, OR: $00CE > 176 = Mario is in the flagpole slide! When Mario slides down the flagpole, his y increases from the top of the pole to the bottom. The pole is ~10 tiles tall (80px). If Mario grabs the pole at y≈90, he slides down to y≈176. Hmm, that's decreasing... no.\n\nHmm. OR: The \"fall\" is Mario falling off the bottom of the screen after the level end (walking into the castle)? No.\n\nHmm, hold on! Let me reconsider: what if $00CE > 176 means Mario is DEAD and in the death animation (the \"jump up then fall\" death)? In SMB when Mario dies (e.g., hit by an enemy), he does a little hop then falls off the screen. The death animation: Mario jumps up, then falls straight down through the floor.\n\nYES! That's exactly the pattern: a small jump (up to y~123) then falls through the floor and off the bottom. That's the DEATH animation!\n\nAnd the lives counter decrements... but we saw $075A stay 9 then go to 8 LATER.\n\nSo Mario is DYING — killed by an enemy (a Goomba), not falling into a pit!\n\nThat explains everything:\n- He's \"stuck\" at 2022 because he died there.\n- The death animation: hop up, then fall.\n- Lives decrement after the animation.\n\nAnd in the list_sprites I saw Goombas right next to Mario (x=71-79, 130-138, 153-161) — one of them at 130-138 is overlapping Mario at 120-135! So Mario got hit by a Goomba.\n\nHmm, but wait — the last list_sprites showed Mario with tiles $9E/$9F which is the DEATH pose? Hmm, actually in SMB the death sprite uses a special tile. Hmm.\n\nBut hold on — in that list_sprites, Mario's entries were at y=177-208 and Goombas at 185-208. And Mario's pose tiles $9E/$9F.\n\nHmm! Actually $9E/$9F IS the \"Mario dying\" sprite! In SMB, when Mario dies he uses the \"dead Mario\" sprite which has arms up. Let me look at $9E: it has index 1 at the top-right (arms up?) and a face. Yeah, plausibly the death pose.\n\nHmm, and actually — wait. In SMB the death animation shows Mario with a distinct \"arms up\" pose. And $9E/$9F could be that.\n\nBUT: the earlier deaths — Mario was at 1249, 1527, 1783, 2024 and \"fell\". If those were enemy deaths, then the enemy killed him.\n\nIn each case there were Goombas around. Plausible.\n\nHmm, but the hop-then-fall pattern: the death animation in SMB is: Mario hops up a bit and falls. Yes!\n\nSo Mario is being KILLED BY GOOMBAS, not falling into pits!\n\nThat changes everything! The ground is continuous (no pits at all in this stretch — and maybe 1-1's pit is elsewhere).\n\nSo my problem is simply: the hop cycle lands Mario next to/on Goombas and he gets hit.\n\nHmm — but with the hop cycle, he should stomp them (landing on top). He got hit side-on.\n\nHmm, and actually: in this last run, at frame ~30-54 Mario was at 2022 and a Goomba at 130-138 (screen) = absolute 2032-2040 was RIGHT next to him. He was blocked... by what? Goombas don't block.\n\nHmm, but he was stuck at 2022 for ~200 frames. A Goomba walking left at 0.5px/f would pass him.\n\nHmm, unless he was in the death animation, which freezes x! YES — during the death animation Mario's x is frozen. So he died at 2022 and his x froze.\n\nBut then $00CE should be increasing the whole time, not showing jump arcs. Hmm. Actually the death animation IS: he hops up once then falls. The watch showed: 161,143,131,124,123 (up), 128,138,154,175 (down to ground), 201,226,251,21 (falling). So ONE hop then fall. That IS the death animation!\n\nAnd in the earlier deaths, I saw similar single-hop-then-fall patterns (or repeated ones due to sampling).\n\nSo: Mario's deaths are from enemies. \n\nWait, but the very first death (the 600-frame run): $00CE showed 176,200,240,24,64,... a long fall with x frozen at 225. Consistent with death animation.\n\nHmm, but in the 600-frame run before that, $00CE showed repeated arcs (a real jump cycle) then... it died eventually.\n\nOK! So: no pits (at least none in this stretch). Great. The problem is Goombas.\n\n**Solution**: stomp or avoid Goombas. With a hop cycle, Mario often lands ON a Goomba (stomp) but sometimes collides side-on. \n\nBetter approach for Goombas: jump HIGH and far so he flies over them, or time jumps. Or: since Goombas are only dangerous at ground level, continuous hopping helps — when he descends onto a Goomba he stomps it.\n\nThe deaths happen when: Mario lands, then a Goomba walks into him while he's grounded (the ~19-frame ground window).\n\nTo minimize: reduce ground time. Or kill the Goombas proactively.\n\nAlternatively: **make Mario invincible** by writing the star timer $079F! The mapping says $079F = star invincibility timer (0-35, ~12s). If I set $079F=35 repeatedly, Mario is invincible and can plow through Goombas!\n\nBut it decrements ~1 per 20 frames; 35 = 700 frames ≈ 11.7s. And it's a RAM write that persists (the game decrements it). I could write it periodically — but play_input calls are long.\n\nHmm, but I can write $079F=35 before a 600-frame run (600 frames = 30 decrements, leaving 5). That covers a whole 600-frame run! \n\nThat's the trick. Let me do that: write $079F = 35 (or higher — is it clamped? it's a countdown byte; setting 255 would give 255*20 frames = 85 seconds!). Let me set it to 200 → 4000 frames of invincibility. \n\nDoes a large value break anything? The routine does `LDA $079F / BNE` — nonzero = invincible. Decrements. Probably safe up to 255.\n\nAlso, while invincible, the star music plays and Mario's palette flashes — that's fine.\n\nLet me try: write_memory 0x079F (address 1951) = 200. Then play.\n\nWait, $079F = 1951 decimal. Yes.\n\nThen Mario can just RUN right through Goombas. And no jumping needed (except for pits/obstacles). That massively simplifies things.\n\nBut careful: invincibility doesn't protect against pits. But we established there may be no pits in this stretch... Actually there might still be pits. But with running on the ground, if there's a pit he'd fall. Hmm.\n\nHmm — but actually, are there pits at all? Let me reconsider: the earlier deaths at 1249, 1527, 1783 had the same \"hop then fall\" death pattern, so probably all enemy deaths too.\n\nGiven the ground row is full everywhere, maybe 1-1 in this ROM has no pits in this region, and the pit is further right.\n\nOK. Plan: \n1. Load state 4 (x=1758) — or better, use the current state? Current is dead-ish. Load 4.\n2. Write $079F = 200 (invincible for ~4000 frames).\n3. Play holding right + B (running) with occasional hops to clear pipes/obstacles, for 600 frames.\n4. Watch $00CE (death), $0086, $006D, $071C, $075A, and $079F.\n5. Repeat, re-writing $079F each time.\n\nWith invincibility, running at 2.5px/f, 600 frames = 1500px. From 1758 → 3258. That should reach the flagpole!\n\nLet me also handle: the staircase (blocks) would block him — he'd need to jump. And the flagpole ends the level.\n\nLet me just run and see.\n\nActually, even better: instead of holding right+B continuously, with invincibility I can just hold right and B and add a jump cycle to clear obstacles. Let me use the hop cycle ({a}1,{b}20) which worked, plus invincibility to survive Goombas.\n\nLet me do it:\n1. load_state 4","truncated":true}
