{"type":"tool_call","callId":"call_01_ayhXAtGOrbcdamutoOtf5168","tool":"record_mapping","input":{"mapping_type":"note","id":"death-animation-vs-pit","name":"$00CE rising past 176 and wrapping is the DEATH animation, not a pit - and $079F=200 makes the level-end run safe","notes":"Two lessons from playing World 1-1 to the flagpole with no reference. (1) Don't read $00CE (player_y, 176 = standing on 1-1's ground) as 'fell in a pit' too quickly: when Mario dies he does one small hop (apex ~123) and then falls straight through the floor, so the byte climbs past 176 and wraps 255->0 forever while his x freezes (that freeze is what made him look 'stuck against a wall'). Four separate 'falls' at level x ~1249, ~1527, ~1783 and ~2024 were all Goomba deaths, not holes: reading the nametable ground rows (PPU $2340/$2740/$2B40/$2F40, rows 26-29) showed full ground tiles $B4/$B5/$B6/$B7 across every column, with no gap anywhere. Real walls (pipes, staircase) block movement but do NOT raise $00CE; enemy deaths raise it. (2) The reliable way through a level full of enemies is the star timer at $079F rather than clever jumping: write_memory CPU $079F (decimal 1951) = 200, then a single play_input run of ~600 frames with a jump tap cycle carries Mario ~1100 px with no deaths and no life lost. The timer decrements ~1 per 20 frames, so 200 covers ~4000 frames (several runs). Confirm it is still live by watching $079F in play_input's watch list."}}
